== 8. adversarial cases of our own design ==
  [PASS] A1 key 5 of 31 bytes -> REFUSED on length, before any tree work  -- version rule: payload key 5 must be 32 bytes, got '31 bytes'
  [PASS] A1 key 5 of 33 bytes -> REFUSED on length, before any tree work  -- version rule: payload key 5 must be 32 bytes, got '33 bytes'
  [PASS] A2a payload keys in DESCENDING order: the signature still verifies (those bytes are what was signed)
  [PASS] A2b ... but its ENCODED keys are in descending order, the deterministic re-encode differs from the published bytes, and our reader refuses it on the key list -- a reader that checked only the signature would accept it  -- encoded keys ['05', '04', '03', '02', '01']; reader says: version rule: content type names v3, which carries exactly payload keys [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], but the payload carries [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]
  [PASS] A3 a payload with key 5 twice: a length-driven decoder accepts the map; RFC 8949 s5.6 calls it invalid, and which copy a dict-building decoder keeps decides the root  -- our decoder surfaces both copies: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 5]
  [PASS] A4 first_seq (11) > last_seq (8): refused by OUR reader, but neither the task text nor the vector states this constraint  -- first_seq (11) > last_seq (8): the reported range is empty or inverted
  [PASS] A5 a statement whose head (record 7) and root (12 records) do not correspond: ACCEPTED without the chain, and the consistency proof still verifies -- nothing in the chainless check binds key 1 to key 5  -- the chainless check is a statement about ROOTS; the head rides along unchecked
  [PASS] A5b ... and a chain-holding verifier catches it only if it checks the head too: the root here IS the right root for last_seq=11  -- root_status='recomputed-ok', head=record 7 while last_seq=11
  [PASS] A6a first == second with an empty path: our faithful RFC 9162 s2.1.4.2 returns False (see finding F1)  -- outside the RFC 9162 s2.1.4.2 input domain 0 < first < second (first=12, second=12)
  [PASS] A6b ... and still False with the domain guard removed, because the RFC's own step 1 fails an empty consistency_path  -- step 1: consistency_path is empty
  [PASS] A7 a root over a different chain entirely: accepted as CARRIED without the chain, rejected with it, and the consistency proof fails -- the proof is what catches it when no chain is held  -- root does not match the derived root over records 0..11
  [PASS] A8 strip tag 18: a tag-requiring parser rejects the artifact while the signature over it is still valid (B1/B2 finding, unchanged in v3)
  [PASS] A9 the task's 'Construction v3' text states leaf/node/promote but omits the empty-tree root that PALA-1 s4.3 and consistency-proof.md s1 both give as SHA-256(""); unreachable here because a payload always has last_seq >= 0, so the tree has >= 1 leaf  -- documentation gap, not a byte divergence; see U3

